104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (Rosalind Franklin started her career as a chemist before becoming a DNA researcher studying Natural sciences at Cambridge University. At a time when women were facing barriers in scientific careers.  She spent years investigating coal and graphite. But Paris changed the course of her life. There, under the guidance of crystallographer Jacques Mering, she developed her expertise in X-ray diffraction. This later on became central to her study of DNA structure. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (THE X IN THE PHOTOGRAPH)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (In the early 1950s, Rosalind Franklin was accepted into King's College London, where she redirected her focus towards learning and studying DNA. She could not see the molecule itself. No microscope could simply show her its shape. DNA was too small, too intricate, too far beyond the reach of ordinary vision. So, she had to find another way. She turned to X-rays.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (THE MAKING OF PHOTOGRAPH  51 )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 55 0 R
/E (When X-rays pass through a carefully prepared sample of DNA, they scatter. Those scattered rays leave behind a pattern on photographic film, and Franklin had spent years learning how to read such patterns. The image, which was number 51 in a series taken with varying humidity and water content, was the best of the high-humidity, "wet," or "B-form" photos. A distinct "A-form" pattern was observed with lower humidity, more akin to the diffraction photos obtained by Maurice Wilkins before she arrives at King's. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
